    build:({request})=>({description:"Hold and release one Session clip.",messages:[userMessage(`Perform this Session clip: ${request}
1. Resolve the exact existing Session track/clip through get_session_orientation, list_tracks and list_clips. Explicit references take precedence over selection; ask one focused question only if materially different targets remain. Read get_clip_timing and get_transport_recording_context. This workflow requires Gate or Repeat, global playback already running under existing authority, recording off, an unarmed/unfrozen target and no pending launches. Do not silently stop a performance, change modes/quantization, disarm recording or unfreeze material. A separately authorized configuration change uses set_clip_timing while stopped, then reads back the actual launchMode.
2. Choose a 0.25–8 second wall-clock duration within the brief. This is checked on native Live display ticks, not beat scheduling or a hard real-time deadline. Quantization can delay activation beyond a short hold, so a press receipt does not prove that a passage played. Use hold_session_clip with exact current IDs and fresh expectedStateVersion. allowPlaying:true is only for an explicitly intended hold on the already-playing target. Another Session clip on its track is refused. Starting Session playback can override that track's Arrangement; no automatic return is made. Native follow actions and other sounding parts remain relevant. Do not change mute/solo, routing, tempo or unrelated parts to make the result audible.
3. Review and confirm the unchanged native plan, retaining the returned holdId. Live requests release even if the client disconnects. Concurrent manual/controller input on this same launch button cannot be distinguished and may be released; do not interleave another held press. No notes, loop settings or launch mode are restored or rewritten. After a failed or lost reply, list get_session_clip_hold without an ID, inspect retained holds and actual clips, and continue only the missing authorized action. Never blindly replay a press. Missing handles after bridge reload do not prove release.
4. Read get_session_clip_hold for the actual handle. Active means a retained press request, not verified playback. Inspect status, pressCallReturned, releaseRequested, releaseCallReturned, isPlaying, isTriggered and cleanupErrors separately. buttonStateVerified remains false: this workflow has no raw held-button readback. Complete means the bounded release call returned; observe actual playback after quantization before describing its effect. Gate release may stop its clip; Repeat may continue playing. Neither release nor handle finishing globally stops playback or stops another clip. Ask what the user hears; snapshots do not prove audible output or rhythmic precision.
5. For an authorized early end use finish_session_clip_hold with cancel:true and a fresh confirmation; it releases only the retained native clip. After cleanup_failed, explicit cancel can retry release against that same handle; inspect the current clip and receipts first. Finish a terminal handle only after accepting its recorded result. A failed release retains the handle and track reservation until an explicit cancel retry succeeds. Finishing a released terminal handle forgets runtime evidence. Preserve manual edits and partial work; do not use global panic, undo, deletion or a replacement press as cleanup. A separate requested stop uses the exact current clip or transport scope. Saving and recording require their own intent. ${confirmWorkflow}`)]})
