    build:({request,mode='collaborative'})=>{
      if(!['collaborative','autonomous'].includes(mode))throw Object.assign(Error('mode must be collaborative or autonomous'),{code:-32602});
      return {description:'Build and inspect a sample instrument.',messages:[userMessage(`Build this sample instrument: ${request}
Working mode: ${mode}. ${mode==='collaborative'?'Resolve materially different sample or target choices with one focused question unless the brief already chooses them.':'Choose a coherent sample and part within the brief, state the choices and continue.'}
1. Read get_session_orientation, get_selection_context, list_tracks, get_transport_recording_context and the target device hierarchy. Resolve the exact ordinary MIDI track, Simpler device (including any rack chain) and intended musical role. Explicit names/IDs take precedence over selection. Find an existing local file through configured local sample search or an explicit path; a browser URI or matching filename is not a filesystem identity. Do not invent a path, download a substitute, or treat catalog metadata as a verified file. Analyze the exact local source when pitch, length or transients matter; source analysis does not prove the loaded instrument's sound.
2. Preserve an existing playable part through prepare_instrument_comparison when its exact MIDI clip is available: resolve the returned original/alternative identities, leave the alternative muted and unarmed, and read both native sample states. If duplication is unavailable, resolve whether a new staging MIDI track or an explicitly authorized in-place replacement is intended. Never silently replace a populated instrument or use exposed-parameter JSON as a complete sample backup. For a new instrument, create the intended MIDI track and load Simpler from an actually observed factory browser path; inspect deviceChainEffect and re-read the hierarchy rather than assuming append or a device ID. Do not create or delete rack chains as an unrequested shortcut.
3. Read get_simpler_sample_state for the exact target. This supports single-sample Simpler, not Sampler or multisample mode. Length, start/end markers and slices are sample frames; divide by the observed sampleRate for source seconds. They are not quarter-note beats or MIDI notes. Choose classic, one-shot or slicing only within the brief. A playback mode does not establish which notes trigger intended pitches or slices. Preserve the source's full sample state, exposed parameters and MIDI notes for later comparison. Resolve stopped transport, recording, arms, input monitoring and pending target launches within existing authority; never stop a take or disarm another track to bypass a refusal.
4. If the chosen sample and mode are already loaded correctly, keep them; a region edit does not require reloading the file. Otherwise plan load_simpler_sample with the actual absolute sourcePath, exact trackId/deviceId, optional playbackMode and fresh expectedStateVersion. allowReplace defaults to false and must be true for a loaded sample only when replacing that exact target is intended, including a chosen comparison alternative. Review the native document/owner/device/sample/parameter identity, controls and file size/time/inode metadata, then confirm the unchanged plan. Replacement can reset sample-derived controls, markers, slices or warp state. Decoded bytes and Live's sample cache are not bound, and there is no complete automatic sample-state rollback. A changed file or native context requires a fresh decision and plan.
5. Require observed.verified and independently re-read get_simpler_sample_state. Check the actual source path, positive frame length/rate, marker bounds, requested mode and exposed settings. Re-read the preserved original's sample/parameters/notes to establish preservation. Do not infer exact hidden state, automation mapping, sound quality or full-file independence from a path or receipt. Refine only the requested exposed device controls with existing guarded parameter tools. For a requested playback region, use set_simpler_sample_region on the exact resolved alternative: provide startMarkerFrames and/or endMarkerFrames from observed source positions, with 0 <= start < end < lengthFrames and fresh expectedStateVersion. Convert source seconds by the observed sampleRate with an explicit frame rounding choice; never use clip beats or normalized parameter guesses. Omitted bounds stay as observed; skip an unchanged region. Confirm the unchanged native context, then require verified and independently read the actual bounds, full sample controls and derived slices/parameters. The tool edits playback markers without cropping the file. Native bounds can change slice availability and normalized sample/loop controls; report those changes and audition. For a complete manual slice layout and a new groove, retrieve chop-sample-groove and use set_simpler_manual_slices; warp edits need their own native support. A partial error requires inspection before continuing, not reloading, global undo or blind replay. For a new part, plan musical notes against the observed instrument and current meter/key, then write only to an exact empty clip slot. Preserve existing notes and resolve sample-root or slice-mapping uncertainty rather than inventing a correct performance.
6. Audition only when requested or already authorized. For a native comparison use its actual handle and select_instrument_comparison to switch the pair's mutes, then launch the resolved part with recording/quantization checks. Do not silently solo, mute unrelated tracks or reroute for isolation; global playback can include other material. Observe actual clip playback and ask what the user hears. Separately requested measurements follow listen-to-live-output with file/provenance checks. Loading a file and playing MIDI do not prove audible output, tuning or musical quality. Keep the original and chosen alternative; restore only owned comparison controls against fresh context.
7. After a lost reply or partial failure, inspect the actual target, sample path, mode and current file version before continuing. Never blindly replay replacement, delete the device, use global undo, or claim metadata can restore a prior sample's full state. Preserve partial work and make uncertainty explicit. Saving, collecting external samples, deleting alternatives and further edits need their own scope. Report the actual sample, target, musical material, observed playback and remaining choices honestly. ${confirmWorkflow}`)]};
    }
